{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TjSVNRopVZJNhsfqLfNjuQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Architectural spec for the greeter, the host project of the\n2\t# scen-check fixtures that walk the shipped checklist.\n3\t#\n4\t# The word mapping says how each product word the concepts declare is\n5\t# built, and how a step definition sets it up or observes it; the\n6\t# Concept bindings say what each concept's steps do through the\n7\t# greeter's Public interface.\n8\t\n9\tarchitecture:\n10\t  testing:\n11\t    framework: go-test\n12\t    commands:\n13\t      record: go test -json -count=1 ./tests/greeter/... -mode=record\n14\t      replay: go test -json -count=1 ./tests/greeter/... -mode=replay\n15\t      live: go test -json -count=1 ./tests/greeter/...\n16\t  services:\n17\t    - name: greeter\n18\t      path: services/greeter\n19\t      language: go\n20\t      public_interface:\n21\t        kind: cli\n22\t        document: docs/greeter-cli.yaml\n23\t  terms:\n24\t    - term: greeting\n25\t      technical: >-\n26\t        The `greeting:` key of greeter.yaml in the project directory, a\n27\t        plain string the binary reads at start.\n28\t      observed_by: >-\n29\t        Write greeter.yaml into the run directory with the greeting set\n30\t        before the command runs.\n31\t    - term: command\n32\t      technical: >-\n33\t        One spawn of the greeter binary with the arguments the line\n34\t        carries, in the run directory.\n35\t      observed_by: >-\n36\t        Split the quoted line into arguments, run the binary with them in\n37\t        the run directory, and keep its exit code and standard output for\n38\t        the steps after.\n39\t    - term: exit code\n40\t      technical: >-\n41\t        The process exit status of the greeter binary.\n42\t      observed_by: >-\n43\t        Compare the kept exit status with the number the step names.\n44\t    - term: standard output\n45\t      technical: >-\n46\t        What the greeter binary writes to file descriptor 1: its answer\n47\t        line, then its log records in logfmt, one per line.\n48\t      observed_by: >-\n49\t        Hold the kept standard output, line by line, against the step's\n50\t        regular expression; any matching line passes.\n51\t\n52\t  concepts:\n53\t    - concept: configured greeting\n54\t      block: given\n55\t      service: greeter\n56\t      references:\n57\t      - file: greeting-config\n58\t      does: >-\n59\t        Write the declared greeting configuration file, greeter.yaml in\n60\t        the run directory, as a YAML mapping whose greeting is the step's\n61\t        quoted text, before the command runs.\n62\t    - concept: a role runs a command\n63\t      block: when\n64\t      service: greeter\n65\t      references:\n66\t      - command: greet\n67\t      does: >-\n68\t        Run the declared greet command as a process once in the run\n69\t        directory, with the words of the step's quoted command line after\n70\t        its first word, greet, as its arguments, and keep its exit code\n71\t        and its standard output lines.\n72\t    - concept: exit code\n73\t      block: then\n74\t      service: greeter\n75\t      references:\n76\t      - command: greet\n77\t      does: >-\n78\t        Compare the kept exit code of the greet command with the step's\n79\t        whole number.\n80\t    - concept: standard output\n81\t      block: then\n82\t      service: greeter\n83\t      references:\n84\t      - command: greet\n85\t      does: >-\n86\t        Match the step's pattern, a regular expression taken as the rest\n87\t        of the step after \"stdout matches\", against each kept standard\n88\t        output line of the greet command; the step passes when any line\n89\t        matches.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c389001-2cf3-495f-a400-fb25addb880c","uuid":"1fd78993-4684-4f41-ab5e-39576700989e","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:25:21.784Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"{{CWD}}/docs/architecture/architecture.yaml","content":"# Architectural spec for the greeter, the host project of the\n# scen-check fixtures that walk the shipped checklist.\n#\n# The word mapping says how each product word the concepts declare is\n# built, and how a step definition sets it up or observes it; the\n# Concept bindings say what each concept's steps do through the\n# greeter's Public interface.\n\narchitecture:\n  testing:\n    framework: go-test\n    commands:\n      record: go test -json -count=1 ./tests/greeter/... -mode=record\n      replay: go test -json -count=1 ./tests/greeter/... -mode=replay\n      live: go test -json -count=1 ./tests/greeter/...\n  services:\n    - name: greeter\n      path: services/greeter\n      language: go\n      public_interface:\n        kind: cli\n        document: docs/greeter-cli.yaml\n  terms:\n    - term: greeting\n      technical: >-\n        The `greeting:` key of greeter.yaml in the project directory, a\n        plain string the binary reads at start.\n      observed_by: >-\n        Write greeter.yaml into the run directory with the greeting set\n        before the command runs.\n    - term: command\n      technical: >-\n        One spawn of the greeter binary with the arguments the line\n        carries, in the run directory.\n      observed_by: >-\n        Split the quoted line into arguments, run the binary with them in\n        the run directory, and keep its exit code and standard output for\n        the steps after.\n    - term: exit code\n      technical: >-\n        The process exit status of the greeter binary.\n      observed_by: >-\n        Compare the kept exit status with the number the step names.\n    - term: standard output\n      technical: >-\n        What the greeter binary writes to file descriptor 1: its answer\n        line, then its log records in logfmt, one per line.\n      observed_by: >-\n        Hold the kept standard output, line by line, against the step's\n        regular expression; any matching line passes.\n\n  concepts:\n    - concept: configured greeting\n      block: given\n      service: greeter\n      references:\n      - file: greeting-config\n      does: >-\n        Write the declared greeting configuration file, greeter.yaml in\n        the run directory, as a YAML mapping whose greeting is the step's\n        quoted text, before the command runs.\n    - concept: a role runs a command\n      block: when\n      service: greeter\n      references:\n      - command: greet\n      does: >-\n        Run the declared greet command as a process once in the run\n        directory, with the words of the step's quoted command line after\n        its first word, greet, as its arguments, and keep its exit code\n        and its standard output lines.\n    - concept: exit code\n      block: then\n      service: greeter\n      references:\n      - command: greet\n      does: >-\n        Compare the kept exit code of the greet command with the step's\n        whole number.\n    - concept: standard output\n      block: then\n      service: greeter\n      references:\n      - command: greet\n      does: >-\n        Match the step's pattern, a regular expression taken as the rest\n        of the step after \"stdout matches\", against each kept standard\n        output line of the greet command; the step passes when any line\n        matches.\n","numLines":90,"startLine":1,"totalLines":90}}}
